This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.

[1]: Snakes have an engulfing attack, which works only against monsters
smaller than them. "The python swallows the woodchuck!" --Grunt

[2]: Wishing for "anything", "something", "surprise me", or "nothing",
should give you either a random item or nothing as appropriate. Wishing
for stereotypical wishes like "peace on earth" should give a funny
message, like "Yeah, me too." --azgor

[3]: Conduct: tame at least one of every tameable monster. Call it the
'Pokemon' conduct: "Gotta catch 'em all!" --Veinor

[4]: An integrated notes page so you can always remember where you left
off when you logged out. --psyklops

[5]: Chromatic dragon scale mail, left when killing Tiamat, to replace
the Sceptre as the Cav quest artifact. Gives all dragon resistances.
--lament

[6]: With the proper items and preparation, your character might manage
to switch to some other role mid-game. Think of the possibilities!
--TheShrewdDude

[7]: Who says you can't ride a purple worm? You shouldn't trust all of
those rumours anyway. With skilled riding, you should be able to ride
pretty much anything. "You piggy-back on the Elvenking. -more-" Maybe
not. --TheShrewdDude

[8]: Negative enchant speed boots slow you down. --Feory

[9]: You should be able to #dip stuff into a pool of water you are
standing next to. --ekiM

[10]: Killing a troll with fire or acid or trollsbane should stop it
from regenerating. --greqrg

[11]: Using one of the *bane artifact weapons against the kind of
monster it is effective against should have a chance in scaring them
into being your slaves (pet). --Xarann

[12]: New conduct: Fearlessness (You never engraved Elbereth.)
--TheShrewdDude

[13]: Comestible: Wafer-thin Mint, always causes choking --tafryn

[14]: Spellbook: the Book of Lies. When read, it produces a fortune
from the false rumour file. (Possible variant effects depending on
B/U/C and Cha) --Fennec

[15]: #force a potion down your pet's throat. (tameness considerations
here.) --Fennec

[16]: Tool: Scales, occasionally dropped by dragons (haha). Applying
them will let you compare the weight of two items, tell whether a grey
stone on the floor is a loadstone (your scales break under the weight!)
and provide a funny message or effect if used while blinfolded.
--Fennec


Individual ideas may have contributions from multiple authors/editors,
